\[ E = -\frac{1}{4\pi \epsilon_0} \frac{\mu^2 \alpha}{r^3} \]

where:
- \( \mu \) is the dipole moment of the peptide group,
- \( \alpha \) is the polarizability volume of the phenyl group,
- \( r \) is the distance between the groups,
- \( \epsilon_0 \) is the permittivity of free space (\( 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{C}^2/\text{N} \cdot \text{m}^2 \)).

Given:
- \( \mu = 1.3 \, \text{D} \) (Debye),
- \( \alpha = 1.04 \times 10^{-29} \, \text{m}^3 \),
- \( r = 4.0 \, \text{nm} = 4.0 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \).

First, convert the dipole moment from Debye to Coulomb meters:
\[ 1 \, \text{D} = 3.33564 \times 10^{-30} \, \text{C} \cdot \text{m} \]
\[ \mu = 1.3 \, \text{D} = 1.3 \times 3.33564 \times 10^{-30} \, \text{C} \cdot \text{m} = 4.336332 \times 10^{-30} \, \text{C} \cdot \text{m} \]

Now, substitute the values into the formula:
\[ E = -\frac{1}{4\pi (8.854 \times 10^{-12})} \frac{(4.336332 \times 10^{-30})^2 (1.04 \times 10^{-29})}{(4.0 \times 10^{-9})^3} \]

Calculate the numerator:
\[ (4.336332 \times 10^{-30})^2 = 1.87999 \times 10^{-59} \]
\[ 1.87999 \times 10^{-59} \times 1.04 \times 10^{-29} = 1.95519 \times 10^{-88} \]

Calculate the denominator:
\[ (4.0 \times 10^{-9})^3 = 6.4 \times 10^{-27} \]

Now, calculate the fraction:
\[ \frac{1.95519 \times 10^{-88}}{6.4 \times 10^{-27}} = 3.055 \times 10^{-62} \]

Finally, calculate the energy:
\[ E = -\frac{1}{4\pi (8.854 \times 10^{-12})} \times 3.055 \times 10^{-62} \]
\[ E = -\frac{1}{1.11265 \times 10^{-10}} \times 3.055 \times 10^{-62} \]
\[ E = -2.746 \times 10^{-53} \, \text{J} \]

To convert this energy to electron volts (1 eV = \( 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J} \)):
\[ E = \frac{-2.746 \times 10^{-53}}{1.602 \times 10^{-19}} \, \text{eV} \]
