 To determine the smallest number of bricks Boris needs to build a cube, we need to find the smallest cube that can be constructed using bricks of dimensions \(1 \text{ cm} \times 2 \text{ cm} \times 3 \text{ cm}\).

First, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the dimensions of the bricks to determine the smallest possible side length of the cube that can be constructed using these bricks.

The dimensions of the bricks are 1, 2, and 3. The LCM of these numbers is the smallest number that is divisible by each of them. 

- The prime factorization of 1 is \(1\).
- The prime factorization of 2 is \(2\).
- The prime factorization of 3 is \(3\).

The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:
\[ \text{LCM} = 2^1 \times 3^1 = 6 \]

So, the smallest cube that can be constructed using these bricks will have a side length of 6 cm.

Next, we calculate the volume of this cube:
\[ \text{Volume of the cube} = 6 \text{ cm} \times 6 \text{ cm} \times 6 \text{ cm} = 216 \text{ cm}^3 \]

Now, we calculate the volume of one brick:
\[ \text{Volume of one brick} = 1 \text{ cm} \times 2 \text{ cm} \times 3 \text{ cm} = 6 \text{ cm}^3 \]

To find the number of bricks needed, we divide the volume of the cube by the volume of one brick:
\[ \text{Number of bricks} = \frac{216 \text{ cm}^3}{6 \text{ cm}^3} = 36 \]

Therefore, the smallest number of bricks Boris would need to build a cube is:
\[ \boxed{36} \]
