2. The area of the triangle outside the circle is \(40\%\) of the total area of the diagram.

Let's denote:
- \(A_{\text{total}}\) as the total area of the diagram.
- \(A_{\text{triangle}}\) as the area of the triangle.
- \(A_{\text{circle}}\) as the area of the circle.
- \(A_{\text{intersection}}\) as the area of the intersection of the triangle and the circle.
- \(A_{\text{triangle outside circle}}\) as the area of the triangle outside the circle.
- \(A_{\text{circle outside triangle}}\) as the area of the circle outside the triangle.

From the problem, we know:
\[ A_{\text{intersection}} = 0.45 \times A_{\text{total}} \]
\[ A_{\text{triangle outside circle}} = 0.40 \times A_{\text{total}} \]

The total area of the triangle can be expressed as:
\[ A_{\text{triangle}} = A_{\text{triangle outside circle}} + A_{\text{intersection}} \]
\[ A_{\text{triangle}} = 0.40 \times A_{\text{total}} + 0.45 \times A_{\text{total}} \]
\[ A_{\text{triangle}} = 0.85 \times A_{\text{total}} \]

Similarly, the total area of the circle can be expressed as:
\[ A_{\text{circle}} = A_{\text{intersection}} + A_{\text{circle outside triangle}} \]

