   \[
   (3 \cdot -2) + (3 \cdot -2) + (-3 \cdot 0) + (-3 \cdot 2) = -6 - 6 + 0 - 6 = -18
   \]

3. Calculate the sum of the products of the coordinates going the other way:
   \[
   (2 \cdot 3) + (-2 \cdot -3) + (-2 \cdot -3) + (0 \cdot 3) = 6 + 6 + 6 + 0 = 18
   \]

4. Subtract the second sum from the first sum and take the absolute value:
   \[
   \left| -18 - 18 \right| = \left| -36 \right| = 36
   \]

5. Divide by 2 to get the area:
   \[
   A = \frac{1}{2} \times 36 = 18
   \]

Thus, the area of quadrilateral \(ABCD\) is \(\boxed{18}\).
